    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/F28471DFA0F4183976163E181A695BE1/jobs/494641": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Annual Giving",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Annual Giving  (81111) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Assistant Director of Annual Giving  (81111) New Rochelle - New Rochelle, NY 10801 Apply Overview Salary Range $70,000.00 - $85,000.00 Salary/year Position Type Full Time Job Shift Day Education Level 4 Year Degree Apply Description The Assistant Director of Annual Giving is responsible for developing, implementing and evaluating strategies designed to increase annual giving participation and philanthropic support for Iona University. Reporting to the Senior Director of Alumni Engagement & Annual Giving, the Assistant Director will play a key role in advancing the University's annual giving priorities through integrated fundraising campaigns, direct marketing, digital fundraising, data analysis, donor segmentation and performance measurement. The Assistant Director will plan and manage all marketing aspects of the Annual Fund program to ensure appropriate solicitation strategies are implemented and that fundraising goals are achieved. This position requires a highly organized, analytical and collaborative professional who can manage multiple priorities, identify opportunities for greater efficiency, and work effectively with colleagues across Advancement and the University. Commitment to Mission: Iona University is a Catholic institution founded in 1940 by the Congregation of Christian Brothers. Candidates must appreciate the history and values of the institution and demonstrate a commitment to its mission of developing graduates “recognized for their ethics, creativity and problem-solving abilities; their independent and adaptable thinking; their joy in lifelong learning; and their enduring integration of mind, body, and spirit\". Responsibilities: Annual Giving Develop and implement strategies to increase the number of donors and level of financial support for Iona University's Annual Fund and other annual giving priorities. Manage key components of the University's integrated annual giving program, including direct mail, email and digital fundraising, online giving initiatives, targeted appeals and other solicitation efforts. Develop and manage a comprehensive annual giving calendar aligned with University and departmental fundraising priorities. Plan and execute annual giving campaigns and appeals, including acquisition, renewal, reactivation, upgrade and targeted donor strategies. Develop creative fundraising materials, including appeal concepts, solicitation language, campaign messaging and other donor-facing materials in collaboration with appropriate colleagues. Coordinate the logistical execution of annual giving solicitations and campaigns, ensuring accuracy, timeliness, appropriate segmentation and cost effectiveness. Develop and implement testing strategies to improve donor acquisition, retention, participation and revenue. Collaborate with Advancement Services to manage data selection, segmentation, tracking, coding and reporting for annual giving programs. Utilize the University's fundraising database and related systems to develop effective donor segments, analyze giving behavior and measure campaign performance. Analyze annual giving trends and results and prepare reports and recommendations for departmental and Advancement leadership. Support efforts to increase alumni participation and strengthen the University's culture of philanthropy. Other duties as assigned. Collaboration & Departmental Support Develop strong working relationships with colleagues throughout Advancement and across the University to advance shared fundraising and engagement objectives. Collaborate with Advancement Communications and other partners to ensure that annual giving campaigns are appropriately integrated with broader University communications and fundraising priorities. Work closely with Advancement Services and other operational partners to ensure the accuracy and effectiveness of fundraising data, processes and reporting. Contribute to department meetings, planning sessions and strategic discussions by providing relevant data, project updates and recommendations. Provide support for other Advancement priorities and initiatives as assigned. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required based on the needs of the University's events and fundraising calendar. Qualifications Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required. At least 2-3 years of progressively responsible experience in annual giving, fundraising, development, alumni relations, advancement operations, project management or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Strong knowledge of annual giving and fundraising principles, with the ability to develop, implement and evaluate fundraising strategies. Excellent project management, organizational and planning skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines and stakeholders. Strong analytical skills, including the ability to interpret fundraising data, identify trends and translate findings into actionable recommendations. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with strong interpersonal and collaborative abilities. Demonstrated initiative, sound judgment and problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify opportunities for process and workflow improvements. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and fundraising databases, with strong attention to detail and accuracy. Preferred Experience: Experience in higher education advancement or a complex nonprofit fundraising environment. Experience managing all aspects of GiveCampus, including campaign setup, configuration, reporting and performance tracking. Experience developing and implementing process improvements or workflow enhancements. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. About Iona/EEO Statement: Founded in 1940, Iona University is a private, Catholic, coeducational institution of learning in the tradition of the Edmund Rice Christian Brothers. Iona is a growing, highly accredited institution located just 20 miles north of Midtown Manhattan, with campuses in New Rochelle, Bronxville, and a new location in County Mayo, Ireland. With over 4,000 students, Iona offers small classes, engaged faculty, and a wide range of programs across the School of Arts & Science, the LaPenta School of Business (AACSB accredited), the NewYork Presbyterian Iona School of Nursing & Health Sciences, and the Hynes Institute for Entrepreneurship & Innovation. Recognized by The Princeton Review, The Wall Street Journal, Forbes, and U.S. News & World Report, Iona is nationally ranked for academic quality, social mobility, and long term return on investment. The University’s continued growth and rising reputation reflect its commitment to academic excellence, innovation, service, and opportunity. Iona University values and celebrates diversity and is committed to providing a welcoming and inclusive environment for all employees and applicants for employment. Iona is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will be considered free from discrimination based on any legally protected characteristic under applicable federal, state, or local laws. Iona University encourages applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/F28471DFA0F4183976163E181A695BE1/jobs/494627": {
      "title": "PT",
      "description": "PT - Flexible Counseling Center Clinician (81074) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account PT - Flexible Counseling Center Clinician (81074) New Rochelle - New Rochelle, NY 10801 Apply Overview Salary Range $50.00 - $75.00 Hourly Position Type Part Time Job Shift Day Education Level Masters Degree Apply Description Iona University Counseling Center is seeking to expand its clinical services to include consulting New York State licensed psychologists, licensed clinical social workers, and licensed mental health counselors. Clinicians will provide in-person individual and group psychotherapy to undergraduate and graduate students on part-time, flexible schedules, to be set in collaboration with the Director of Counseling. The position may also include occasional provision of single sessions during walk-in hours. Schedules are limited to business hours, Monday-Friday, 9-5, during the academic year (fall semester late August – mid December, spring semester mid-January – mid May), excluding university holidays. A consistent schedule of 8-16 hours per week, or similar, is available. Hours are compensated regardless of client attendance of scheduled appointments. Clinicians must provide all services in person, at the Iona University Counseling Center located on Iona’s New Rochelle campus. Licensed clinicians seeking to supplement private practices, or who may currently be employed part-time in other settings, may be particularly interested in these positions. Responsibilities To provide clinical services including individual and group psychotherapy; crisis intervention; risk assessment; consultation and referral; walk-in triage; case management of clients. To document all professional activities promptly in the Counseling Center’s Electronic Health Record system in accordance with professional standards and Counseling Center policies. To participate in regular meetings with Counseling Center leadership to consult on clinical matters and track progress. To contribute to policy and reporting documents as assigned. To perform additional duties to be assigned by the Counseling Center administration as deemed necessary. Qualifications Clinicians with competency in specific modalities are needed, including CBT, DBT, ACT, ERP, IFS, and motivational interviewing. Specialized expertise with issues common in college populations is welcome, including substance use, eating and body image, neurodivergence, mood disorders, and adjustment disorders. Prior clinical experience with emerging adults is required. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. About Iona/EEO Statement: Founded in 1940, Iona University is a private, Catholic, coeducational institution of learning in the tradition of the Edmund Rice Christian Brothers. Iona is a growing, highly accredited institution located just 20 miles north of Midtown Manhattan, with campuses in New Rochelle, Bronxville, and a new location in County Mayo, Ireland. With over 4,000 students, Iona offers small classes, engaged faculty, and a wide range of programs across the School of Arts & Science, the LaPenta School of Business (AACSB accredited), the NewYork Presbyterian Iona School of Nursing & Health Sciences, and the Hynes Institute for Entrepreneurship & Innovation. Recognized by The Princeton Review, The Wall Street Journal, Forbes, and U.S. News & World Report, Iona is nationally ranked for academic quality, social mobility, and long term return on investment. The University’s continued growth and rising reputation reflect its commitment to academic excellence, innovation, service, and opportunity. Iona University values and celebrates diversity and is committed to providing a welcoming and inclusive environment for all employees and applicants for employment. Iona is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will be considered free from discrimination based on any legally protected characteristic under applicable federal, state, or local laws. Iona University encourages applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/F28471DFA0F4183976163E181A695BE1/jobs/494559": {
      "title": "Staff Psychologist, Neurodivergence Specialist",
      "description": "Staff Psychologist, Neurodivergence Specialist (81092) You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Staff Psychologist, Neurodivergence Specialist (81092) New Rochelle - New Rochelle, NY 10801; Bronxville - Bronxville, NY 10708; Remote - New Rochelle, NY 10801 Apply Overview Salary Range $85,000.00 - $95,000.00 Salary/year Position Type Full Time Education Level Doctoral Degree Apply Description The Iona University Counseling Center seeks a Staff Psychologist, Neurodivergence Specialist to join its close-knit and experienced clinical team. This position combines direct clinical care with program development, psychoeducation, consultation, training, and community building focused on supporting neurodivergent college students. The Specialist is a core member of the Counseling Center and reports to the Executive Director. The position offers an opportunity to develop specialized expertise while helping shape and expand neurodivergence-focused services at Iona. Essential Responsibilities Clinical Services Provide individual and group psychotherapy to neurodivergent and other assigned student clients. Provide walk-in, triage, crisis intervention, emergency evaluation, risk assessment, consultation, and referral services. Participate in the Counseling Center's on-call rotation and provide crisis consultation as assigned. Maintain timely and accurate clinical documentation. Neurodivergence Programming and Student Engagement Lead and further develop Gael Chat Social Communication, including curriculum development, program delivery, management, and evaluation. Develop and deliver psychoeducational groups, workshops, and community-building programs for neurodivergent students. Serve as an advisor to the University's Neurodiversity Club. Create psychoeducational materials and collaborate with students and campus partners to identify emerging needs and develop responsive programming. Training, Consultation, and Supervision Provide neurodivergence-focused training, workshops, seminars, and case consultation to Counseling Center colleagues and campus partners. Consult with faculty, staff, administrators, and student leaders regarding student mental health and neurodivergence. Provide clinical supervision to graduate externs and fellows, as assigned. Professional and University Responsibilities Contribute to Counseling Center operations, policies, reports, and initiatives. Participate in relevant University and Division of Enrollment Management & Student Affairs meetings and events. Perform other related duties as assigned. Why Join Iona? Iona offers the opportunity to build a rewarding career within a vibrant and growing university community. As a member of the Counseling Center, you will join an experienced, collaborative clinical team that values consultation, professional growth, and high-quality care. This position offers opportunities to: Develop expertise in neurodivergence and college mental health. Provide individual and group psychotherapy to a diverse student population. Develop innovative programs and psychoeducational initiatives. Teach, train, consult, and collaborate with campus partners. Provide clinical supervision to emerging clinicians. Enjoy generous vacation time, university holidays, and a comprehensive benefits package. Work Schedule This is an onsite position with a general business hours schedule, with participation in a scheduled rotation of limited remote hours. The Counseling Center is closed on university holidays. As an exempt role, it is expected that the Specialist will remain present outside normal business hours occasionally, for crisis intervention and required campus events, and that they participate by phone in the on-call rotation. Benefits Iona University offers a comprehensive benefits package, including generous vacation time in addition to university holidays, medical, dental, and vision insurance, 401(k) matching, tuition remission, and other benefits. About the Counseling Center The Counseling Center provides accessible, responsive, and high-quality mental health services that support student well-being and success. Staff work within a collaborative clinical community with opportunities to strengthen clinical and supervisory skills, develop specialized expertise, contribute to campus initiatives, and participate in the broader life of the University. If you are a psychologist who is passionate about working with college students, experienced in supporting neurodivergent individuals, and energized by the opportunity to combine clinical work with teaching, consultation, and program development, we encourage you to apply. Qualifications The successful candidate will: Hold a doctoral degree in psychology from an APA-accredited program. Be licensed in New York State as a clinical, counseling, or school psychologist. Have demonstrated experience providing psychotherapy to neurodivergent individuals, including individuals on the autism spectrum. Have experience addressing anxiety, attention, and executive functioning concerns. Demonstrate established clinical skills in CBT, DBT, ACT, and/or other evidence-based approaches relevant to this population. Demonstrate strong interpersonal, communication, consultation, and collaboration skills. Be comfortable working collaboratively with students, colleagues, faculty, staff, and administrators. Bring curiosity, creativity, and enthusiasm to clinical practice and program development. Practice ethically and in accordance with the highest professional standards at all times. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. About Iona/EEO Statement: Founded in 1940, Iona University is a private, Catholic, coeducational institution of learning in the tradition of the Edmund Rice Christian Brothers. Iona is a growing, highly accredited institution located just 20 miles north of Midtown Manhattan, with campuses in New Rochelle, Bronxville, and a new location in County Mayo, Ireland. With over 4,000 students, Iona offers small classes, engaged faculty, and a wide range of programs across the School of Arts & Science, the LaPenta School of Business (AACSB accredited), the New York Presbyterian Iona School of Nursing & Health Sciences, and the Hynes Institute for Entrepreneurship & Innovation. Recognized by The Princeton Review, The Wall Street Journal, Forbes, and U.S. News & World Report, Iona is nationally ranked for academic quality, social mobility, and long term return on investment. The University’s continued growth and rising reputation reflect its commitment to academic excellence, innovation, service, and opportunity. Iona University values and celebrates diversity and is committed to providing a welcoming and inclusive environment for all employees and applicants for employment. Iona is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will be considered free from discrimination based on any legally protected characteristic under applicable federal, state, or local laws. Iona University encourages applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    }
